up|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I think 1N is North's best bid here, though the hand is at the top of the range for 1N. With one more point, North should bid 2N (showing 11-12). Also, a few things to discuss on both declarer play and the defense.|
PG| ***** 70 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It was a reasonable plan for North to try and set up diamonds. However, with that suit combination, at trick two leading the @DQ was incorrect because it can never gain a trick, even if the finesse works, as this hand illustrates. |
PG| ***** 71 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The best way to play diamonds is to lead a low diamond to the @DA and then lead a low diamond back to the @DQ hoping West has the @DK. That is the correct way to take the diamond finesse. As it turns out it doesn't make any difference but if West did hold the @DK it would have.|
PG| ***** 72 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As to the last few tricks, North chose to play for down 1 which given she was vulnerable, was reasonable if playing Match Points. The difference in a part score hand between -100 and -200 in Match Points is huge.|
PG| ***** 73 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|-200 is always a very bad result on a part score whereas -100 is often average or even a bit above average. However, in IMPs you want to play to make your contract. So if playing IMPs she should take the club finesse. Of course, then she ends up going down 3. In IMPs that is worth the risk.|
PG| ***** 74 ***** |


